course: everyday
lesson: 349
title: Going where you're not allowed
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Some rules guard your body, not your manners. Tom in Sydney thought his bike helmet looked babyish and left it at home. He rode without it three times and nothing happened, so he thought nothing ever would. The fourth time he fell and scraped his head and had to see the doctor. Three safe rides did not make the fourth one safe. He wears his helmet every time now.
explain: Tom found matches in a drawer and struck one to see the flame. He burned his finger and frightened himself, and nobody else knew. He held the finger under cold water, but the fright and the guilt stayed. That evening he told Dad, who was very serious with him; on Saturday he told it at confession too, and walked home lighter, with a promise never to touch them again.
explain: At the beach Tom swam out past the flags to show off to his brother. The lifeguard whistled him back, and the family had to leave early. The flags mark the safest water the lifeguards are watching; outside them there may be rip currents that pull swimmers out to sea.
explain: Other rules seem small but are there for a reason: the oven door Mum says is hot really is hot, the big knife needs to be shown and practised with a grown-up, a strange dog off its lead can run into a road, and a scary film made for older people can give a younger child nightmares for a week.
fact: A bike helmet protects your head in a fall, and falls can happen on any ride.
fact: Getting away with something risky several times does not make it safe the next time.
fact: Matches and lighters are for grown-ups; children should leave them alone and tell an adult if they find them.
fact: A burn should be cooled under cool running water for about 20 minutes.
fact: At beaches with lifeguards, swim between the red and yellow flags, where lifeguards are watching.
fact: Rip currents can pull swimmers away from the shore, even strong swimmers.
fact: Age ratings on films (a number like 12 or 15: the youngest age the film is meant for) are there because some films can frighten younger children badly.
fact: Coming in when the street lights come on is a promise to the people waiting for you.
fact: Sharp knives should be used only with a grown-up's permission and help.
fact: Never touch or unclip a stranger's dog without the owner's permission.
exercise: Why did Tom leave his helmet at home? | He thought it looked babyish | It was broken and wouldn't clip | His Dad said he didn't need it | He forgot where he had put it
exercise: Tom rode without a helmet three times safely. What does this show about the fourth ride? | Nothing: a fall can happen on any ride | The fourth ride is sure to be safe, because the first three were | Helmets are only needed on long rides | He had learned not to fall
exercise: What happened on the fourth ride? | He fell, scraped his head and saw the doctor | A friend lent him a helmet just before he fell off | He won a race | Nothing happened
exercise: Tom found matches in a drawer. What should a child do with matches he finds? | Leave them and tell a grown-up | Strike one outside, where it's safe to see | Hide them in his room for later | Give them to a friend to look after
exercise: What should you do first for a small burn on your finger? | Cool it under cool running water for about 20 minutes | Put ice straight on it for an hour, the colder the better for a burn | Put butter on it | Cover it and ignore it
exercise: Nobody knew about the matches. Why did Tom tell Dad anyway? | The fright and the guilt stayed, and Dad needed to know | He wanted to show off his burn to Dad and get a big plaster for it | He wanted more matches | Dad already saw
exercise: Why are the red and yellow flags on a beach important? | They mark the water the lifeguards are watching | They show where the sharks have been seen that day | They show the deepest water | They're just decoration
exercise: What can happen outside the flags? | A rip current can pull a swimmer out to sea | The water is always calmer there, away from the crowds | Nothing different | The sand gets hot
exercise: A girl watches a scary film she isn't allowed to, on a dare. What happens? | She has nightmares for a week | She becomes braver at once and sleeps well | She sleeps better than ever before | Nothing at all happens to her after
exercise: Why are there age ratings on films? | Some films can frighten or upset younger children | To sell more tickets to grown-ups, who pay more than children | Because children can't read | To stop grown-ups watching cartoons
exercise: A boy doesn't come in when the street lights come on. Dad has to find him. What was broken? | A promise to the people waiting for him | A street light, nothing more than that | The front door | Nothing important
exercise: A boy touches the oven door after Mum says it's hot, because he didn't believe her. What does he learn? | Grown-ups usually say hot for a reason | Touching quickly is safe, it takes a while to burn | Oven doors are never hot | Mum was exaggerating
exercise: A girl uses the big knife without asking and cuts her finger. What is the safe fix? | Ask a grown-up to show her how to use it safely | Use the big knife faster, so it is over before she can slip | Hide the plaster | Never eat apples again
exercise: A boy unclips a stranger's dog because he feels sorry for it. It runs off. What should he have done? | Leave the dog alone unless the owner says yes | Hold the lead tighter after unclipping it from the collar | Let it off only near roads | Give it a treat first
exercise: Which is the wise choice when friends dare you to swim out past the flags? | Stay between the flags and say no | Swim out a little way only, then come back | Swim out if the lifeguard looks away | Swim out with a friend who is good at it
test: Mate: «I never wear a helmet and I've never fallen once». What's true? | That doesn't mean the next ride is safe | Then he doesn't need one, he's a good rider | Helmets are for babies | Only fast riders need one
test: Little brother: «found matches in the drawer, wanna see?» What should you do? | Say no, leave them, and tell a grown-up | Strike one outside, away from the house | Hide them in your room | Show your friends
test: Lifeguard's whistle, then a shout: «Back between the flags, mate!» What should the boy do? | Swim back between the flags at once | Wave and stay out there, it's fun | Swim further out | Pretend not to hear
test: Cousin: «it's only a 15, don't be a baby, we watched one last week and nothing happened». What's wise? | Say no; the rating is there for a reason | Watch it with eyes half shut at the scary bits | Watch only the start | Watch it if nobody tells
test: Mum: «Don't touch, that oven door's HOT». The boy thinks she's exaggerating. What's wise? | Believe her and keep his hands away | Touch it quickly to check | Touch it with one finger | Wait till she turns round
test: Dad, worried: «street lights came on half an hour ago». What does this mean for the boy? | He broke his promise to come in, and Dad was worried | It doesn't matter in summer, when it stays light until very late | Dad was joking | Street lights don't mean anything
test: Tom at confession: «I played with matches and burned myself». Father: «Have you told your father?» Why does it matter? | Dad needs to know so Tom stays safe | It doesn't matter any more after confession | Dads must punish everything they find out | So Dad can hide the matches from himself
test: Girl: «I'll just use the big knife, Mum's busy». What's the wise thing? | Wait and ask Mum to help | Use it quickly | Use it with her eyes closed | Use the bread knife instead without asking
test: Owner, running: «WHO let my dog off?!» The boy did, trying to be kind. What should he do? | Own up, say sorry and help look for the dog | Say the dog slipped out by itself under the gate | Run home | Blame another child
test: Which is TRUE about rules about safety? | They protect your body, even if they look babyish | They don't matter if you're careful and ride slowly enough | They're only for little children | You can skip them on holiday
test: Kid with burned finger: «it's fine, I'll put butter on it». What's right? | Cool it under cool running water instead | Butter is the best thing | Ice cubes straight on it for an hour | Leave it alone
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning repeated cycling without a helmet in the absence of injury. | Previous uneventful journeys afford no protection on any subsequent journey | Previous uneventful journeys demonstrate that a helmet is unnecessary for a competent rider | Helmets are required solely upon long journeys | The risk of injury diminishes with each uneventful journey
exam: Identify the principal purpose of the red and yellow flags displayed upon a patrolled beach. | They indicate the area supervised by lifeguards and judged safest for bathing | They indicate the presence of dangerous marine animals, such as sharks and jellyfish, nearby | They indicate the deepest water available for swimming | They serve purely ornamental purposes
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the appropriate first treatment of a minor burn? | The burn ought to be cooled beneath cool running water for approximately twenty minutes | The burn ought to be covered with butter | Ice ought to be applied directly for a prolonged period | The burn ought to be left without treatment
exam: Determine the proper conduct of a child who discovers matches within a household drawer. | He ought to leave them undisturbed and inform an adult | He ought to strike one in order to examine the flame | He ought to conceal them in his own room | He ought to give them to another child
